## 2. Literature Review

This project is anchored in six decades of inventory theory, bridging classical operations research with modern data-driven simulation and machine learning.

### 2.1 The Theoretical Foundation of (s,S) Policies

The continuous-review (s, S) inventory policy, where an order is placed to raise inventory to level `S` whenever it falls to or below `s`, has its theoretical origins in the seminal work of **Arrow, Harris, and Marschak (1951)** , who first formulated the stochastic inventory problem with fixed ordering costs. **Scarf (1960)** provided the definitive proof of optimality for the (s, S) policy in a single-product, stochastic-demand setting using the concept of K-convexity. **Iglehart (1963)** later extended this result to infinite-horizon problems, establishing the policy's long-run average cost optimality. 

More recently, **Perera and Sethi (2022)** provided a comprehensive survey of discrete-time stochastic inventory models, confirming that (s, S) and its variants remain the gold standard for systems with fixed ordering costs, backlogging, and Markovian demand. This body of work justifies our exclusive focus on the (s, S) policy over simpler base-stock or (R, Q) heuristics.

### 2.2 Stochastic Demand and Lead-Time Modelling

While classical models often assume deterministic lead times, real-world supply chains are subject to significant variability. **You, Wang, and Li (2026)** explicitly addressed this gap by developing a data-driven simulation-optimization framework for (s, S) policies under joint demand and lead-time uncertainty. Their approach—which uses Monte Carlo simulation to evaluate the compound distribution of lead-time demand $D_t^L = \sum_{i=t}^{t+L-1} D_i$ and a Pareto frontier for multi-objective trade-offs—served as the **architectural blueprint** for our pipeline.

Furthermore, **Tai, Huyen, and Buddhakulsomsiri (2021)** demonstrated that mean-based approximations (such as the Hadley-Whitin method) consistently underestimate on-hand inventory and overestimate storage requirements in discrete-time systems. Their findings motivated our use of discrete weekly periods and empirical demand sampling directly from forecast residuals, rather than relying on closed-form normal approximations.

### 2.3 Forecasting in Supply Chains

Accurate demand forecasting is a prerequisite for effective inventory management. **Taylor and Letham (2018)** introduced Prophet, a decomposable time-series model designed for business forecasting. Prophet explicitly handles non-stationary trends, multiple seasonalities (weekly, yearly), and holiday effects, making it particularly suited for retail data. **Makridakis, Spiliotis, and Assimakopoulos (2018)** , in their seminal M4 forecasting competition, demonstrated that hybrid approaches (statistical + machine learning) often outperform pure black-box methods when seasonality is strong. Our benchmarking of Prophet against XGBoost directly tests this hypothesis in the Walmart context.

### 2.4 Multi-Echelon and Emerging AI Contexts

While our project focuses on a single-echelon (store-level) system, the broader literature situates this problem within more complex networks. **Ahmadi et al. (2019)** developed a centralized stochastic (s, S) inventory model for perishable products in a multi-echelon supply chain (central warehouse to hospitals), incorporating age-dependent pricing. This work provides a compelling extension for future research, particularly in cold chain logistics.

Finally, **Shakya, Lee, and Ng (2022)** explored a model-free Deep Reinforcement Learning (DQN) approach for inventory control under stochastic lead times, achieving performance comparable to (R, S) policies. Their work highlights a potential future direction for our pipeline, though we opted for the interpretability and mathematical tractability of simulation-optimization in this study.

