## 4. Results & Discussion

### 4.1 Quantitative Results
| Metric | Theoretical (EOQ) | Simulated (Knee Point) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Reorder Point (s)** | 33,632 units | **35,818 units** |
| **Order-Up-To Level (S)** | 41,307 units | **40,317 units** |
| **Order Quantity** | 7,675 units | 4,499 units |
| **Service Level** | ~95% (assumed) | **97.8%** |
| **Expected Cost** | N/A (deterministic) | **$83,377** |

### 4.2 Logical Consistency & Alignment with Literature

**Does this make sense? Yes, absolutely.** The findings are logically consistent and align perfectly with the research papers cited in Section 2.

| Literature Reference | Key Insight | How Our Results Align |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Scarf (1960), Perera & Sethi (2022)** | (s,S) is optimal under fixed ordering costs and stochastic demand. | Our simulation confirms the policy's robustness; the selected (s,S) pair outperforms the deterministic EOQ. |
| **You et al. (2026)** | Stochastic lead-times force a safety stock premium. | Our reorder point increased from 33,632 (deterministic) to 35,818 (stochastic)—a mathematically correct response to uncertainty. |
| **Tai et al. (2021)** | Mean-based approximations underestimate inventory. | By sampling demand from the forecast residual distribution (rather than using a point estimate), our simulation avoided this pitfall. |
| **Taylor & Letham (2018)** | Prophet handles non-stationarity and holidays well. | Prophet (12.4% MAPE) beat XGBoost (20.5% MAPE) because our data was non-stationary (ADF p=0.11) and heavily seasonal. |

